# Every stage directory is a Docker build context and every stage Dockerfile
# copies the whole of it into the image, so anything left lying about here gets
# baked in.  Nothing below is a file the stage needs: it is the debris that
# accumulates from running the suite locally, plus editor backups.  The same
# list ships in all three stage directories -- a stage that excluded something
# the others kept would produce an image nobody could reproduce from the tree.
#
# Every pattern is prefixed `**/`, and that prefix is the whole point of this
# file.  `.dockerignore` matches a pattern against the entire context-relative
# path and does not re-apply it at each directory level the way `.gitignore`
# does, so a bare `__pycache__/` excludes a `__pycache__` sitting in this
# directory and nothing deeper -- not `lib/__pycache__`, not
# `modules/closure/__pycache__`, which are the only two places running the
# suite locally ever puts one.  Measured, not reasoned: with the unprefixed
# form, planting a `.pyc` in each of those two directories and building put
# both of them in the image.
#
# `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1` in the Dockerfile looks like it covers this and
# does not.  It stops the image from writing bytecode; it has no bearing on
# COPY bringing the author's bytecode in.  The difference is visible only in
# the interpreter tag on the filename -- a `cpython-310` tag on an image whose
# own `python -V` says 3.11 came from the host -- which is why the Dockerfile
# also asserts, after its last bytecode-writing step, that none shipped.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/*~
**/*.orig
**/*.rej
**/.DS_Store
